{"id":"mohcd-records-counted-twice","title":"Housing office service records: one in ten is a second copy","question":"Do the housing office's published service records count each service once?","finding":"In 82 of 322 activity-years of the published records, every record has an identical twin (same activity, year, age band, income band, race group, neighbourhood and client number): 66,228 records, 33,114 of them second copies, 10.6% of all 311,824. In the other activity-years a typical 3% of such cells hold an even count and none more than 50%. The office's own reports to HUD print half the paired count for 13 of 13 paired activity-years tested, and the full count for 10 of 10 unpaired controls.","how":"Records grouped by program area, activity and fiscal year; inside each, counted by race group x income band x age band x neighbourhood x client number. A group is \"paired\" when it has at least 5 such cells and at least 95% of them hold an even count. The CAPER test looks for half (paired) or all (controls) of the records among the whole numbers printed beside the matching output indicator in the 2017-18 and 2018-19 CAPER texts.","limits":"The published file may hold one row per service per funding source or per reporting period, which would pair rows with no double counting in the office's books; the HUD reports use the lower figure, so the reports to the funder are not inflated. The records carry no grantee, so the pairs cannot be traced to an organization. Nothing here is about any client.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"program","t":"Program area","type":"text"},{"k":"activity","t":"Activity","type":"text"},{"k":"fy","t":"Fiscal year","type":"text"},{"k":"records","t":"Records published","type":"int"},{"k":"distinct","t":"Once each","type":"int"},{"k":"cells","t":"Distinct cells","type":"int"}],"rows":[{"program":"Housing Place-Based Services","activity":"Service Connection","fy":"2018-19","records":3104,"distinct":1552,"cells":842},{"program":"Service Connec